<section class="mt-4" aria-labelledby="about-heading">
  <h2 id="about-heading">About</h2>

  <p>This site lets you explore Stanford Libraries' digital collections in two ways.</p>

  <ul>
  <li><strong>Search</strong> finds items for you to browse. It goes beyond exact keywords and also matches on meaning or semantic similarity. Each result shows the passages that matched your search. You can use the filters to narrow your results. An AI-written summary at the top of the page gives an overview of the results you're looking at.</li>

  <li><strong>Ask AI</strong> answers your questions directly. Ask a question in plain language, such as <em>"What did students say about the band's halftime shows in the 1970s?"</em> The assistant searches the collections, reads the most relevant passages and writes an answer, with citations that link back to the original items. You can also start from a search: click <strong>Ask AI about these results</strong> to bring your query, filters and results into the conversation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="h6">Which should I use?</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Use <strong>Search</strong> when you want to browse, compare items, or find a specific title, name or phrase.</li>
    <li>Use <strong>Ask AI</strong> when you have questions about specific people, places, themes, or documents and want a detailed answer with sources.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
